{
  "assessment": "The paper provides a microscopic theory for nuclear spin thermodynamics in systems with long electron spin correlation times, deriving quantitative expressions for inverse nuclear spin temperature and electron-induced nuclear spin-lattice relaxation rate as functions of external magnetic field and correlation time. It includes explicit equations, parameter values (e.g., Phi=3/2 short, 1/2 long), and numerical results for specific N_I values. The source supports numeric controls (magnetic field B, correlation time tau_c), readouts (inverse spin temperature beta, heating rate 1/T_E), and quantitative mechanisms (Eqs. beta, TE, Phi). Case authoring is ready.",
  "case_authoring_ready": true,
  "source": {
    "citation": "D. S. Smirnov et al., Cooling and Heating Nuclear Spins by Strongly Localized Electrons, Physical Review Letters (2025), DOI:10.1103/physrevlett.134.016201",
    "journal_doi": "10.1103/physrevlett.134.016201",
    "arxiv_versioned_id": "2406.08296v2"
  },
  "paper_scope": "The paper develops a microscopic theory of dynamic nuclear spin polarization and relaxation by strongly localized electrons in quantum dots, bridging the spin temperature concept and central spin model. It derives expressions for inverse nuclear spin temperature and electron-induced nuclear spin-lattice relaxation rate as functions of external magnetic field and electron correlation time, and analyzes limits of short and long correlation times.",
  "mechanisms": [
    {
      "mechanism_id": "inverse_spin_temperature",
      "statement": "The inverse nuclear spin temperature established under dynamic polarization by electrons is given by Eq. (eq:beta), which depends on the external magnetic field B, electron mean spin projection, correlation time tau_c, and the coefficient Phi.",
      "equations": [
        "beta = 4 <S_z> B / (hbar gamma_N) / (B^2 + Phi * (T_{1,e}^N / tau_c) * (tau_c / T_{2,e}^*)^2 * B_L^2)"
      ],
      "variable_names": ["beta", "B", "<S_z>", "gamma_N", "Phi", "T_{1,e}^N", "tau_c", "T_{2,e}^*", "B_L"],
      "parameters": [
        {
          "name": "Phi_short",
          "value": 1.5,
          "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "Coefficient Phi in the short correlation time limit",
          "anchors": [
            {"line_start": 134, "line_end": 134, "support": "In the limit of short correlation time, Eq. (eq:T_eN) reduces to T_{1,e}^N=1/(<Omega_{e,x}^2+Omega_{e,y}^2> tau_c), so Eq. (eq:beta) reproduces the classical result of the Dyakonov-Perel theory, to which end one should put Phi=3/2."}
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "Phi_long",
          "value": 0.5,
          "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "Coefficient Phi in the long correlation time limit",
          "anchors": [
            {"line_start": 136, "line_end": 136, "support": "In the opposite limit of long correlation time, Phi then becomes equal to 1/2."}
          ]
        }
      ],
      "anchors": [
        {"line_start": 127, "line_end": 132, "support": "Substituting Eqs. (eq:q_Z) and (eq:q_SS) in Eq. (eq:balance) and making use of Eq. (eq:P_N) we obtain the inverse spin temperature established under dynamic polarization by electrons: beta = 4 <S_z> B/(hbar gamma_N) / (B^2 + Phi * (T_{1,e}^N/tau_c) * (tau_c/T_{2,e}^*)^2 * B_L^2), where T_{2,e}^* = hbar/(a sqrt(N_I)) with N_I = 4 N I(I+1)/3."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "mechanism_id": "heating_rate",
      "statement": "The electron-induced nuclear spin-lattice relaxation rate (heating rate) in the dark is given by Eq. (eq:TE), which depends on magnetic field B, correlation time tau_c, and the coefficient Phi.",
      "equations": [
        "1/T_E = f / (N_I (B^2 + B_L^2)) * (B^2 / T_{1,e}^N + Phi * tau_c * B_L^2 / T_{2,e}^{*2})"
      ],
      "variable_names": ["T_E", "f", "N_I", "B", "B_L", "T_{1,e}^N", "Phi", "tau_c", "T_{2,e}^*"],
      "parameters": [],
      "anchors": [
        {"line_start": 168, "line_end": 183, "support": "In the dark regime, the dynamics of the inverse spin temperature is determined by warming up of Zeeman and spin-spin energy reservoirs: dot{beta} = (q_Z + q_{SS}) / (partial E_{NSS}/partial beta), with heat capacity -partial E_{NSS}/partial beta = N_I (hbar gamma_N/2)^2 (B^2 + B_L^2). This leads to the general expression for the electron-induced nuclear spin-lattice relaxation rate: 1/T_E = f / (N_I (B^2 + B_L^2)) * (B^2 / T_{1,e}^N + Phi * tau_c * B_L^2 / T_{2,e}^{*2})."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "mechanism_id": "phi_coefficient",
      "statement": "The coefficient Phi, which quantifies the heating of the nuclear spin-spin reservoir, is given by Eq. (eq:Phi_ans) as a function of the electron spin precession frequency and correlation time.",
      "equations": [
        "Phi = <1/(1 + Omega_e^2 tau_c^2)> + 1/2"
      ],
      "variable_names": ["Phi", "Omega_e", "tau_c"],
      "parameters": [],
      "anchors": [
        {"line_start": 150, "line_end": 155, "support": "Making use of the separation of the time scales of electron and nuclear spin dynamics we find Phi = <1/(1 + Omega_e^2 tau_c^2)> + 1/2. One can see that it equals 3/2 for short correlation times, and decreases 3 times with increase of tau_c."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "mechanism_id": "optimal_field",
      "statement": "The optimal magnetic field B_0 at which the maximum of the inverse spin temperature is reached is given by Eq. (eq:B0_anal) for large number of nuclei and long correlation times.",
      "equations": [
        "B_0 = (1/T_{2,e}^*) / sqrt(gamma_e^2 + 4/(tau_c * tilde{B}_L)^2)"
      ],
      "variable_names": ["B_0", "T_{2,e}^*", "gamma_e", "tau_c", "tilde{B}_L"],
      "parameters": [],
      "anchors": [
        {"line_start": 159, "line_end": 163, "support": "For a large number of nuclei, N_I >> (T_{2,e}^* gamma_e B_L)^{-2}, and at long correlation times, tau_c >> T_{2,e}^*, we obtain B_0 = (1/T_{2,e}^*) / sqrt(gamma_e^2 + 4/(tau_c * tilde{B}_L)^2). This dependence is shown by the black dashed line in Fig. (c) and agrees well with the red curve."}
      ]
    }
  ],
  "fixed_conditions": [
    {
      "condition_id": "high_temp_approx",
      "statement": "The high-temperature approximation is used for the nuclear spin system.",
      "anchors": [
        {"line_start": 78, "line_end": 78, "support": "Hereafter we use the high-temperature approximation."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "box_approx",
      "statement": "The hyperfine interaction is assumed to be homogeneous (the 'box' approximation).",
      "anchors": [
        {"line_start": 148, "line_end": 148, "support": "The hyperfine interaction is assumed to be homogeneous (the 'box' approximation)."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "small_polarization",
      "statement": "The nuclear spin polarization is assumed to be much smaller than 1/sqrt(N).",
      "anchors": [
        {"line_start": 157, "line_end": 157, "support": "Eqs. (eq:T_eN) and (eq:Phi_ans) allow one to calculate the nuclear spin temperature from Eq. (eq:beta), for arbitrary correlation time assuming the nuclear spin polarization much smaller than 1/sqrt(N)."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "neglect_other_relax",
      "statement": "Other sources of electron spin relaxation are neglected, as they are less important at low temperatures and magnetic fields.",
      "anchors": [
        {"line_start": 95, "line_end": 95, "support": "We neglect the other sources of the electron spin relaxation, which are less important at low temperatures and magnetic fields."}
      ]
    }
  ],
  "controls": [
    {
      "name": "B",
      "definition": "External magnetic field applied along the z axis.",
      "unit": "T",
      "minimum": 0,
      "maximum": null,
      "anchors": [
        {"line_start": 73, "line_end": 73, "support": "It defines all the quantities pertinent to the NSS and, in particular, the mean nuclear spin in the magnetic field B applied along the z axis."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "tau_c",
      "definition": "Correlation time during which an electron stays at a localization site.",
      "unit": "s",
      "minimum": 0,
      "maximum": null,
      "anchors": [
        {"line_start": 80, "line_end": 80, "support": "We assume that the electrons with the mean spin <S> come to the given localization site (quantum dot) with the periodicity tau_R and stay during a correlation time tau_c so that the filling factor of the quantum dots equals f = tau_c/tau_R."}
      ]
    }
  ],
  "readouts": [
    {
      "name": "beta",
      "definition": "Inverse nuclear spin temperature established under dynamic polarization by electrons.",
      "unit": "1/J",
      "statistic": "steady-state value",
      "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
      "anchors": [
        {"line_start": 127, "line_end": 131, "support": "Substituting Eqs. (eq:q_Z) and (eq:q_SS) in Eq. (eq:balance) and making use of Eq. (eq:P_N) we obtain the inverse spin temperature established under dynamic polarization by electrons: beta = ..."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "heating_rate",
      "definition": "Electron-induced nuclear spin-lattice relaxation rate (inverse of T_E) in the dark.",
      "unit": "1/s",
      "statistic": "rate",
      "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
      "anchors": [
        {"line_start": 177, "line_end": 180, "support": "This leads the following general expression for the electron-induced nuclear spin-lattice relaxation rate: 1/T_E = ..."}
      ]
    }
  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim_id": "beta_short_tau",
      "statement": "In the limit of short correlation time, the inverse nuclear spin temperature follows the classical Dyakonov-Perel result with Phi = 3/2.",
      "quantitative_form": "beta = 4 <S_z> B/(hbar gamma_N) / (B^2 + (3/2) * (T_{1,e}^N/tau_c) * (tau_c/T_{2,e}^*)^2 * B_L^2)",
      "mechanism_ids": ["inverse_spin_temperature"],
      "control_names": ["B", "tau_c"],
      "readout_names": ["beta"],
      "fixed_condition_ids": ["high_temp_approx", "box_approx", "small_polarization"],
      "applicability": "Short correlation time limit, tau_c << T_{2,e}^*.",
      "statistical_basis": "Theoretical derivation from detailed balance and spin temperature concept.",
      "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
      "depends_on": [],
      "anchors": [
        {"line_start": 134, "line_end": 134, "support": "In the limit of short correlation time, Eq. (eq:T_eN) reduces to T_{1,e}^N=1/(<Omega_{e,x}^2+Omega_{e,y}^2> tau_c), so Eq. (eq:beta) reproduces the classical result of the Dyakonov-Perel theory, to which end one should put Phi=3/2."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "beta_long_tau",
      "statement": "In the limit of long correlation time, the inverse nuclear spin temperature is given by Eq. (eq:betalong) with Phi = 1/2.",
      "quantitative_form": "beta = 4 <S_z> B/(hbar gamma_N) / (B^2 + (tau_c/T_{2,e}^*)^2 * B_L^2 / 4)",
      "mechanism_ids": ["inverse_spin_temperature"],
      "control_names": ["B", "tau_c"],
      "readout_names": ["beta"],
      "fixed_condition_ids": ["high_temp_approx", "box_approx", "small_polarization"],
      "applicability": "Long correlation time limit, tau_c >> T_{2,e}^*.",
      "statistical_basis": "Theoretical derivation from the general expression with Phi = 1/2.",
      "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
      "depends_on": [],
      "anchors": [
        {"line_start": 136, "line_end": 140, "support": "In the opposite limit of long correlation time, Phi then becomes equal to 1/2. The electron spin relaxation time by nuclei in this limit equals 3 tau_c/2, which reflects the fact that the electron loses 2/3 of its spin polarization with each coming to a new localization center. Thus, in the limit of long correlation time, the inverse nuclear spin temperature is beta = 4 <S_z> B/(hbar gamma_N) / (B^2 + (tau_c/T_{2,e}^*)^2 * B_L^2 / 4)."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "heating_rate_short",
      "statement": "At short correlation time, the heating rate has a Lorentzian form and decreases thrice with increase of magnetic field beyond B_L.",
      "quantitative_form": "1/T_E = f / (N_I (B^2 + B_L^2)) * (B^2 / T_{1,e}^N + (3/2) * tau_c * B_L^2 / T_{2,e}^{*2})",
      "mechanism_ids": ["heating_rate"],
      "control_names": ["B", "tau_c"],
      "readout_names": ["heating_rate"],
      "fixed_condition_ids": ["high_temp_approx", "box_approx", "small_polarization"],
      "applicability": "Short correlation time limit, tau_c << T_{2,e}^*.",
      "statistical_basis": "Theoretical derivation from Eq. (eq:TE).",
      "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
      "depends_on": [],
      "anchors": [
        {"line_start": 191, "line_end": 191, "support": "The heating rate (normalized to T_0^{-1}=f tau_c/(2 N_I T_{2,e}^{*2})) is shown in Fig. (heating) as a function of magnetic field. As well as the dynamic polarization efficiency, the heating rate at short correlation time (black line) follows the classical theory: It has a Lorentzian form and decreases thrice with increase of magnetic field beyond B_L."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "heating_rate_long",
      "statement": "For longer correlation times, the heating rate at zero magnetic field decreases thrice, but changes much more than three times with magnetic field.",
      "quantitative_form": "1/T_E = f / (N_I (B^2 + B_L^2)) * (B^2 / T_{1,e}^N + (1/2) * tau_c * B_L^2 / T_{2,e}^{*2})",
      "mechanism_ids": ["heating_rate"],
      "control_names": ["B", "tau_c"],
      "readout_names": ["heating_rate"],
      "fixed_condition_ids": ["high_temp_approx", "box_approx", "small_polarization"],
      "applicability": "Long correlation time limit, tau_c >> T_{2,e}^*.",
      "statistical_basis": "Theoretical derivation from Eq. (eq:TE) with Phi = 1/2.",
      "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
      "depends_on": [],
      "anchors": [
        {"line_start": 191, "line_end": 191, "support": "For longer correlation times, the heating rate at zero magnetic field decreases thrice, but most importantly it changes much more than three times with magnetic field."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "optimal_field_behavior",
      "statement": "The optimal magnetic field B_0 grows linearly with tau_c and saturates at 1/(gamma_e T_{2,e}^*) for large N_I, and saturates at sqrt(2 N_I/5) tilde{B}_L for smaller N_I.",
      "quantitative_form": "B_0 = (1/T_{2,e}^*) / sqrt(gamma_e^2 + 4/(tau_c * tilde{B}_L)^2) for large N_I; B_0 saturates at sqrt(2 N_I/5) tilde{B}_L for smaller N_I",
      "mechanism_ids": ["optimal_field"],
      "control_names": ["B", "tau_c"],
      "readout_names": ["beta"],
      "fixed_condition_ids": ["high_temp_approx", "box_approx", "small_polarization"],
      "applicability": "Long correlation time limit, tau_c >> T_{2,e}^*.",
      "statistical_basis": "Theoretical derivation and numerical results shown in Fig. (combo).",
      "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
      "depends_on": [],
      "anchors": [
        {"line_start": 159, "line_end": 164, "support": "For a large number of nuclei, N_I >> (T_{2,e}^* gamma_e B_L)^{-2}, and at long correlation times, tau_c >> T_{2,e}^*, we obtain B_0 = (1/T_{2,e}^*) / sqrt(gamma_e^2 + 4/(tau_c * tilde{B}_L)^2). This dependence is shown by the black dashed line in Fig. (c) and agrees well with the red curve. In particular, B_0 grows linearly with tau_c and saturates at 1/(gamma_e T_{2,e}^*). For smaller number of nuclei, B_0 saturates at sqrt(2 N_I/5) tilde{B}_L [blue dotted line in Fig. (c)]."}
      ]
    }
  ],
  "source_ambiguities": [
    {
      "ambiguity_id": "Phi_long_vs_Phi_ans",
      "statement": "The main text states Phi = 1/2 in the long correlation time limit, but the supplemental material derives Phi = (4/5)(hbar/(a tau_c))^2 for long times, which is qualitatively different. The main text's Phi_ans gives 1/2, while the supplemental gives a different expression.",
      "claim_ids": ["beta_long_tau"],
      "anchors": [
        {"line_start": 136, "line_end": 136, "support": "In the opposite limit of long correlation time, Phi then becomes equal to 1/2."},
        {"line_start": 892, "line_end": 897, "support": "For long correlation time, when at/(2hbar) >> 1 one can neglect the oscillating terms in Eq. (eq:ESS_phi), which gives Phi = (4/5)(hbar/(a tau_c))^2 in a qualitative agreement with the general considerations in the main text."}
      ]
    }
  ]
}
